  "id": "DrugBank_DB00846 - MONDO_6547",
  "verbalization": "Flurandrenolide, a corticosteroid, is implicated in the pathogenesis of Exanthem (disease) through a series of interconnected indications. The evidence suggests that Flurandrenolide may be used to treat Lichen Disease and Seborrheic Dermatitis, both of which are inflammatory conditions that can lead to exanthem. The mechanism of action likely involves the suppression of inflammatory responses, as corticosteroids inhibit the production of pro-inflammatory mediators such as cytokines and histamines. This suppression could contribute to the reduction of skin inflammation, which is a common feature of exanthem. The presence of multiple pathways linking Flurandrenolide to exanthem underscores the potential for its use in managing inflammatory disorders that manifest as skin eruptions. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of corticosteroid therapy in modulating immune responses, which may be a key mechanism underlying its therapeutic effect in exanthem.",
